DATA={'V/09': {'intro': 'Modules generalize vector spaces by allowing scalars from an arbitrary ring. Exact sequences record how modules are assembled from submodules and quotients and become the basic grammar of homological algebra.', 'sections': [('Modules', 'An $A$-module is an abelian group with compatible scalar multiplication by $A$.'), ('Submodules and quotients', 'Submodules are additive subgroups stable under scalars; quotient modules encode congruence modulo a submodule.'), ('Module maps', 'Kernels, images, and cokernels are modules and satisfy the familiar first isomorphism theorem.'), ('Exact sequences', 'A sequence is exact when the image at each term equals the next kernel.'), ('Short exact sequences', "$0\\to M'\\to M\\to M''\\to0$ expresses $M'$ as a submodule and $M''$ as the corresponding quotient."), ('Splittings', 'A short exact sequence splits when the middle module is isomorphic to a direct sum of its ends.'), ('Direct sums', 'Direct sums collect finitely supported tuples and implement coproducts in the module category.'), ('Diagram arguments', 'Exact sequences organize kernel-image calculations and support diagram lemmas later in homological algebra.'), ('Examples', 'Ideals, quotient modules, free modules, torsion abelian groups, and vector spaces all fit the same language.')], 'theorems': [('First isomorphism theorem for modules', 'For an $A$-linear map $f:M\\to N$, $M/\\ker f\\cong\\operatorname{im}f$.', 'Send $m+\\ker f$ to $f(m)$. The same kernel argument as for groups and rings gives a well-defined bijective linear map onto the image.'), ('Splitting criterion', "A short exact sequence $0\\to M'\\xrightarrow{i}M\\xrightarrow{p}M''\\to0$ splits iff $p$ has a section, equivalently iff $i$ has a retraction.", "A section $s$ gives $M=i(M')\\oplus s(M'')$. Conversely a direct-sum decomposition supplies both projection and inclusion maps realizing the section and retraction."), ('Exactness of direct sums', 'Taking arbitrary direct sums of short exact sequences of modules preserves exactness.', 'All maps act componentwise. An element of a direct sum has finite support, so kernel and image membership can be checked component by component.')]}, 'V/10': {'intro': 'The tensor product converts bilinear maps into linear maps. It controls base change, localization, flatness, and many multilinear constructions throughout algebra and geometry.', 'sections': [('Balanced bilinear maps', 'An $A$-balanced map satisfies $b(am,n)=b(m,an)$ in addition to additivity in each variable.'), ('Tensor product', '$M\\otimes_A N$ is generated by symbols $m\\otimes n$ modulo additivity and balancing relations.'), ('Universal property', 'Balanced bilinear maps out of $M\\times N$ correspond uniquely to linear maps out of $M\\otimes_A N$.'), ('Basic identities', '$A\\otimes_A M\\cong M$ and direct sums commute with tensor products.'), ('Quotient tensors', '$(A/I)\\otimes_A M\\cong M/IM$.'), ('Associativity and symmetry', 'Canonical isomorphisms identify iterated tensor products and swap factors over a commutative ring.'), ('Right exactness', 'Tensoring preserves cokernels and surjections, but not necessarily injections.'), ('Localization', '$S^{-1}A\\otimes_A M\\cong S^{-1}M$.'), ('Examples', 'Cyclic groups, quotient modules, field extensions, and polynomial base change exhibit torsion and dimension phenomena.'), ('Multilinear extension', 'Exterior and symmetric powers are built by further quotienting tensor powers.')], 'theorems': [('Universal property', 'For every balanced bilinear map $b:M\\times N\\to P$, there is a unique linear map $\\widetilde b:M\\otimes_A N\\to P$ with $\\widetilde b(m\\otimes n)=b(m,n)$.', 'Construct the tensor product as the free module on pairs modulo the subgroup generated by additivity and balancing relations. The relations are exactly those killed by every balanced bilinear map.'), ('Quotient tensor identity', 'For an ideal $I\\subset A$, $(A/I)\\otimes_A M\\cong M/IM$.', 'Send $(a+I)\\otimes m$ to $am+IM$. The map is balanced and surjective; the universal properties on both sides show that its kernel is precisely the relations generated by $I M$.'), ('Right exactness of tensor', "If $M'\\to M\\to M''\\to0$ is exact, then $M'\\otimes N\\to M\\otimes N\\to M''\\otimes N\\to0$ is exact.", 'The tensor product is a left adjoint in either variable, so it preserves cokernels; directly, generators in the kernel of the final map differ by tensors coming from the preceding module.')]}, 'V/11': {'intro': 'Quotients and base change describe how algebraic relations behave after changing the scalar ring. Tensor products are the mechanism that transports modules, presentations, and exact sequences to the new base.', 'sections': [('Scalar extension', 'For $A\\to B$, the base-changed module is $B\\otimes_A M$.'), ('Restriction of scalars', 'Every $B$-module is an $A$-module by composing scalar actions with $A\\to B$.'), ('Quotient base change', '$B\\otimes_A(A/I)$ is naturally $B/IB$.'), ('Submodule caution', 'Base change need not preserve injections unless the new scalar module is flat.'), ('Presentations', 'Base changing a presentation replaces its matrices by the same matrices with entries mapped into $B$.'), ('Localization as base change', 'Localization is scalar extension along $A\\to S^{-1}A$.'), ('Residue-field fibers', '$M\\otimes_A\\kappa(\\mathfrak p)$ measures the fiber of a module at a prime.'), ('Iterated base change', 'For $A\\to B\\to C$, there is a canonical isomorphism $C\\otimes_B(B\\otimes_A M)\\cong C\\otimes_A M$.'), ('Compatibility with quotients', 'Cokernels commute with base change because tensor is right exact.')], 'theorems': [('Quotient under base change', 'For $A\\to B$ and ideal $I\\subset A$, $B\\otimes_A A/I\\cong B/IB$.', 'Apply the quotient tensor identity to the $A$-module $B$; the submodule $IB$ is generated by images of elements of $I$.'), ('Transitivity of base change', 'For $A\\to B\\to C$ and an $A$-module $M$, $C\\otimes_B(B\\otimes_A M)\\cong C\\otimes_A M$.', 'Send $c\\otimes(b\\otimes m)$ to $cb\\otimes m$. Balancedness gives a well-defined map, and the inverse sends $c\\otimes m$ to $c\\otimes(1\\otimes m)$.'), ('Base change of a cokernel', "If $M'\\to M\\to Q\\to0$ is exact, then $B\\otimes_A M'\\to B\\otimes_A M\\to B\\otimes_A Q\\to0$ is exact.", 'This is the right exactness of tensor product with the $A$-module $B$.')]}, 'V/12': {'intro': 'Hom and finite presentation control maps out of modules and the stability of algebraic data under limits and base change. Finitely presented modules are those described by finitely many generators and finitely many relations.', 'sections': [('Hom modules', 'For $A$-modules $M,N$, $\\operatorname{Hom}_A(M,N)$ is an $A$-module under pointwise operations.'), ('Contravariance and covariance', 'Hom is contravariant in its first argument and covariant in its second.'), ('Left exactness', '$\\operatorname{Hom}_A(M,-)$ preserves kernels but not generally surjections.'), ('Finite generation', 'A module is finitely generated if a finite free module surjects onto it.'), ('Finite presentation', 'A module is finitely presented if it is the cokernel of a map between finite free modules.'), ('Generators and relations', 'A finite presentation packages finitely many generators together with finitely many module relations.'), ('Hom and filtered colimits', 'Finite presentation is characterized by commuting of Hom out of the module with filtered colimits.'), ('Base change of presentations', 'Finite presentations remain finite after scalar extension.'), ('Localization of Hom', 'For finitely presented $M$, localization commutes with $\\operatorname{Hom}_A(M,N)$.'), ('Matrix viewpoint', 'Maps between finite free modules are matrices, so finite presentations translate module questions into matrix algebra.')], 'theorems': [('Finite presentation criterion', '$M$ is finitely presented iff there exists an exact sequence $A^m\\to A^n\\to M\\to0$ with finite $m,n$.', 'This is the definition translated into generators and relations: the second map chooses finitely many generators and the image of the first supplies finitely many generators for all relations.'), ('Hom is left exact', "For $0\\to N'\\to N\\to N''$, the sequence $0\\to\\operatorname{Hom}(M,N')\\to\\operatorname{Hom}(M,N)\\to\\operatorname{Hom}(M,N'')$ is exact.", "A map into $N$ lands in $N'$ exactly when its composite with $N\\to N''$ is zero. Injectivity follows because $N'\\to N$ is injective."), ('Localization of Hom for finitely presented modules', 'If $M$ is finitely presented, then $S^{-1}\\operatorname{Hom}_A(M,N)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}_{S^{-1}A}(S^{-1}M,S^{-1}N)$.', 'Choose a finite presentation of $M$, apply Hom to it, localize the resulting kernel description, and use exactness of localization together with the evident formula for finite free modules.')]}, 'V/13': {'intro': 'Free modules have bases, while projective modules are direct summands of free modules. Projectivity is exactly the lifting property that makes Hom out of the module preserve surjections.', 'sections': [('Free modules', 'A free module is a direct sum of copies of the ring with a distinguished basis.'), ('Rank', 'Over suitable rings, finite free modules have a well-defined rank; locally, projective modules acquire ranks prime by prime.'), ('Projective modules', 'A module is projective if maps to quotients lift across surjections.'), ('Direct summands', 'Projective modules are exactly direct summands of free modules.'), ('Idempotent matrices', 'Finite projective modules arise as images of idempotent endomorphisms of finite free modules.'), ('Splitting exact sequences', 'A surjection onto a projective module splits.'), ('Hom exactness', '$P$ is projective iff $\\operatorname{Hom}(P,-)$ is exact.'), ('Localization', 'Localization preserves projectivity, and finite projective modules are locally free.'), ('Examples', 'Free modules, ideals generated by idempotents, and vector bundles over affine rings illustrate projectivity.')], 'theorems': [('Projective summand criterion', 'An $A$-module $P$ is projective iff it is a direct summand of a free module.', 'Choose a free module $F$ surjecting onto $P$. If $P$ is projective, the surjection splits, so $F\\cong P\\oplus K$. Conversely direct summands of free modules inherit the lifting property.'), ('Splitting criterion', 'A module $P$ is projective iff every short exact sequence $0\\to K\\to M\\to P\\to0$ splits.', 'Projectivity lifts the identity map of $P$ through the surjection $M\\to P$, producing a section. Conversely apply the splitting property to pullbacks of arbitrary surjections.'), ('Hom exactness criterion', '$P$ is projective iff $\\operatorname{Hom}_A(P,-)$ is an exact functor.', 'Hom is always left exact. Exactness on surjections is precisely the lifting property defining projectivity.')]}, 'V/14': {'intro': 'Flat modules are those whose tensor product functor preserves injections and hence all finite exact sequences. Flatness is the homological condition behind well-behaved base change.', 'sections': [('Definition of flatness', 'An $A$-module $F$ is flat if $-\\otimes_A F$ is exact.'), ('Injection test', 'Because tensor is always right exact, flatness is equivalent to preserving injections.'), ('Free and projective modules', 'Free modules are flat, and direct summands of flat modules are flat; hence projectives are flat.'), ('Localization', 'Every localization $S^{-1}A$ is flat over $A$.'), ('Filtered colimits', 'Filtered colimits of flat modules are flat.'), ('Ideal criterion', 'Flatness can be tested by injectivity of $I\\otimes F\\to F$ for finitely generated ideals under standard hypotheses.'), ('Tor preview', '$\\operatorname{Tor}_1^A(-,F)$ measures the failure of flatness.'), ('Faithful flatness', 'A flat module is faithfully flat when tensoring also detects nonzero modules.'), ('Base change', 'Flat scalar extension preserves short exact sequences and scheme-theoretic fiber behavior.'), ('Examples and failures', 'Quotient modules are generally not flat; localization provides the basic positive example.')], 'theorems': [('Free implies flat', 'Every free $A$-module is flat.', 'Tensoring with a direct sum of copies of $A$ gives the corresponding direct sum of the original sequence. Direct sums preserve exactness in modules.'), ('Projective implies flat', 'Every projective module is flat.', 'A projective module is a direct summand of a free module. Tensoring with the corresponding direct-sum decomposition shows the tensor functor for the projective summand is a direct summand of an exact functor and hence exact.'), ('Localization is flat', '$S^{-1}A$ is flat as an $A$-module.', 'Tensoring with $S^{-1}A$ is localization of modules, and localization is exact.')]}, 'V/15': {'intro': 'Noetherian conditions impose finiteness on ideals and submodules. They are stable under quotients and finite-type polynomial extension and support induction arguments throughout algebraic geometry.', 'sections': [('ACC on submodules', 'A module is Noetherian when every ascending chain of submodules stabilizes.'), ('Finite-generation criterion', 'A module is Noetherian iff every submodule is finitely generated.'), ('Noetherian rings', 'A ring is Noetherian when it is Noetherian as a module over itself, equivalently every ideal is finitely generated.'), ('Exact sequences', 'In a short exact sequence, the middle module is Noetherian iff both ends are Noetherian.'), ('Finite modules', 'Finite modules over a Noetherian ring are Noetherian.'), ('Quotients', 'Quotients of Noetherian rings and modules are Noetherian.'), ('Hilbert basis theorem', 'If $A$ is Noetherian, then $A[x]$ is Noetherian.'), ('Finite-type algebras', "Iterating Hilbert's theorem shows finitely generated $A$-algebras are Noetherian."), ('Noetherian induction', 'Properties can be proved by assuming all strictly larger subobjects satisfy them.'), ('Examples', 'Fields, PID-like rings, polynomial rings over fields, and many coordinate rings are Noetherian.')], 'theorems': [('Finite-generation criterion', 'An $A$-module is Noetherian iff every submodule is finitely generated.', 'If every submodule is finite, the union of an ascending chain is finitely generated and therefore contained in one stage. Conversely, if a submodule were not finitely generated, repeatedly adjoining new elements would produce a strictly increasing chain.'), ('Exact-sequence criterion', "For $0\\to M'\\to M\\to M''\\to0$, $M$ is Noetherian iff $M'$ and $M''$ are Noetherian.", "Submodules of $M'$ and images in $M''$ control any chain in $M$. For the converse, submodules and quotients of a Noetherian module are Noetherian."), ('Hilbert basis theorem', 'If $A$ is Noetherian, then $A[x]$ is Noetherian.', 'For an ideal $I\\subset A[x]$, leading coefficients form an ideal of $A$ and stabilize under finite generation. Choose finitely many polynomials controlling high-degree leading terms and finitely many lower-degree remainders; together they generate $I$.')]}, 'V/16': {'intro': 'The support of a module records the primes where the module remains nonzero after localization. It turns module-theoretic information into a subset of the prime spectrum and behaves well under exact sequences.', 'sections': [('Definition', '$\\operatorname{Supp}M=\\{\\mathfrak p:M_{\\mathfrak p}\\ne0\\}$.'), ('Annihilator', 'The annihilator $\\operatorname{Ann}(M)$ consists of scalars killing every element of $M$.'), ('Finite-module support', 'For finitely generated $M$, $\\operatorname{Supp}M=V(\\operatorname{Ann}M)$.'), ('Cyclic modules', '$\\operatorname{Supp}(A/I)=V(I)$.'), ('Exact sequences', 'Support of the middle term in a short exact sequence is the union of supports of the ends.'), ('Direct sums', 'Support of a direct sum is the union of the supports.'), ('Tensor products', 'Support of a tensor product is contained in the intersection of supports and equals it under common finite-generation hypotheses over local rings.'), ('Localization', 'Support localizes by restricting to primes contained in the chosen localization domain.'), ('Vanishing detection', 'A module is zero iff all localizations at maximal ideals are zero.'), ('Geometric role', 'Support marks the locus where a sheaf-like algebraic object is present.')], 'theorems': [('Support of a finite module', 'If $M$ is finitely generated, then $\\operatorname{Supp}M=V(\\operatorname{Ann}M)$.', 'If an annihilator element becomes a unit at $\\mathfrak p$, the localized module vanishes. Conversely, if $M_{\\mathfrak p}=0$, finitely many generators are each killed by denominators outside $\\mathfrak p$; their product lies in $\\operatorname{Ann}M$ but outside $\\mathfrak p$.'), ('Support in a short exact sequence', "For $0\\to M'\\to M\\to M''\\to0$, $\\operatorname{Supp}M=\\operatorname{Supp}M'\\cup\\operatorname{Supp}M''$.", 'Localize the sequence. Exactness of localization shows the middle localization is zero exactly when both end localizations are zero.'), ('Maximal-local detection', 'An $A$-module $M$ is zero iff $M_{\\mathfrak m}=0$ for every maximal ideal $\\mathfrak m$.', 'If $m\\ne0$, its annihilator is proper and lies in some maximal ideal $\\mathfrak m$. Then $m/1$ cannot vanish in $M_{\\mathfrak m}$, otherwise an element outside $\\mathfrak m$ would annihilate $m$.')]}, 'V/17': {'intro': 'Associated primes identify prime annihilators of individual module elements. They refine support by detecting the primes that occur as embedded or minimal algebraic components.', 'sections': [('Definition', 'A prime $\\mathfrak p$ is associated to $M$ if $\\mathfrak p=\\operatorname{Ann}(m)$ for some $m\\in M$.'), ('Cyclic submodules', 'Associated primes arise exactly from embedded copies of $A/\\mathfrak p$ inside $M$.'), ('Existence in Noetherian modules', 'Every nonzero finitely generated module over a Noetherian ring has an associated prime.'), ('Containment in support', 'Every associated prime belongs to the support.'), ('Minimal support primes', 'Minimal primes in the support of a finite module are associated.'), ('Exact sequence behavior', 'Associated primes of ends and middle terms satisfy useful inclusions.'), ('Localization', 'Associated primes localize by keeping precisely those contained in the localization prime.'), ('Zero divisors', 'For finite modules over Noetherian rings, the zero divisors on $M$ are the union of associated primes.'), ('Primary decomposition preview', 'Associated primes index components that later appear in primary decompositions.')], 'theorems': [('Associated prime existence', 'If $M\\ne0$ is finitely generated over a Noetherian ring, then $M$ has an associated prime.', 'Choose an annihilator maximal among annihilators of nonzero elements. If $ab$ lies in it and $b$ does not, the annihilator of $bm$ contains the chosen annihilator and also $a$; maximality forces $a$ into the original annihilator, proving primality.'), ('Associated implies support', 'If $\\mathfrak p\\in\\operatorname{Ass}M$, then $\\mathfrak p\\in\\operatorname{Supp}M$.', 'An embedding $A/\\mathfrak p\\hookrightarrow M$ localizes to a nonzero embedding at $\\mathfrak p$, since $(A/\\mathfrak p)_{\\mathfrak p}$ is nonzero.'), ('Minimal support primes are associated', 'For a finitely generated module over a Noetherian ring, every prime minimal in $\\operatorname{Supp}M$ lies in $\\operatorname{Ass}M$.', 'Localize at the minimal prime. The localized support has only the maximal prime, so an associated prime of the nonzero localized module must be that maximal prime; contract back.')]}, 'V/18': {'intro': 'I-adic topology and completion encode infinite approximation by powers of an ideal. Completion turns compatible residue classes modulo $I^n$ into actual elements and is fundamental for local algebra and formal geometry.', 'sections': [('I-adic filtration', 'The powers $M\\supset IM\\supset I^2M\\supset\\cdots$ define progressively finer congruences.'), ('I-adic topology', 'Cosets of $I^nM$ form neighborhoods of module elements.'), ('Separatedness', 'The topology is separated when $\\bigcap_n I^nM=0$.'), ('Completion', '$\\widehat M=\\varprojlim_n M/I^nM$ consists of compatible systems of residues.'), ('Ring completion', '$\\widehat A$ is a ring and $\\widehat M$ naturally a module over it.'), ('Universal approximation', 'A completion element determines all finite-order approximations simultaneously.'), ('Examples', '$p$-adic integers and formal power-series rings arise as completions.'), ('Krull intersection preview', 'Under Noetherian local hypotheses, intersections of powers often vanish.'), ('Exactness issues', 'Completion is not always exact, but for finite modules over Noetherian rings it behaves much better.'), ('Comparison with tensor', 'For finitely generated modules in the Noetherian setting, completion often agrees with tensoring by $\\widehat A$.')], 'theorems': [('Inverse-limit description', 'The I-adic completion of $M$ is the inverse limit $\\varprojlim M/I^nM$.', 'A Cauchy-compatible system is exactly a sequence of residue classes whose class modulo $I^nM$ agrees with every later approximation. This is the defining inverse-limit condition.'), ('Completion map kernel', 'The kernel of the natural map $M\\to\\widehat M$ is $\\bigcap_{n\\ge1}I^nM$.', 'An element maps to zero in the inverse limit exactly when it is zero in every quotient $M/I^nM$, equivalently when it lies in every $I^nM$.'), ('Power-series completion', 'The $(x)$-adic completion of $k[x]$ is canonically $k[[x]]$.', 'A compatible sequence modulo $x^n$ uniquely specifies all coefficients of a formal power series, and truncating a power series gives a compatible residue system.')]}}
